        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "1f2bc67c-900d-5689-a455-e79f8ef5ef75",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Concepts: Short interspersed nuclear elements (SINEs), RNA polymerase III, ribosomes, ribosomal proteins, translation\n- RNA Types: ribosomal RNA (rRNA), transfer RNA (tRNA), messenger RNA (mRNA)\n- Systems: small ribosomal subunit, large ribosomal subunit\n\nFacts:\n- Short interspersed nuclear elements (SINEs) are transcribed by RNA polymerase III.\n- RNA polymerase III is known to transcribe ribosomal RNA (rRNA) and transfer RNA (tRNA).\n- Ribosomal RNA (rRNA) and transfer RNA (tRNA) are vital to ribosomal assembly and messenger RNA (mRNA) translation.\n- Ribosomes consist of a small ribosomal subunit and a large ribosomal subunit.\n- The small ribosomal subunit reads RNA.\n- The large ribosomal subunit joins amino acids to form a polypeptide chain.\n- Ribosomal proteins, together with ribosomal RNA (rRNA), make up the ribosomal subunits involved in cellular translation.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
